# First Piece — Plio (Working Draft)

> **Status:** Draft v1 (Looking for direction, not polish)

## Purpose

This is my first attempt at turning a Canonical into a story.

The direction I'm exploring is:

- Start from a philosophy already present on the website.
- Use a real client engagement to make that philosophy tangible rather than explaining it directly.
- This is intentionally rough. I'm sharing it early so the thinking can be shaped before the writing is polished.

---

# Preserving Clarity as Products Grow
If you're building software that people genuinely depend on, there comes a point where the conversations begin to change.

Nothing has failed.

The product works.

People use it every day.

New features continue to ship.

From the outside, everything appears healthy.

Yet something begins to feel different.

In one of the early conversations around Plio, someone paused and said something that stayed with us.

"It's not that anything is broken… it's just getting harder to explain how all of this fits together now."

That sentence wasn't describing a bug.

It wasn't describing performance.

It wasn't even describing technology.

It was describing the growing distance between the product and the team's shared understanding of it.

As Plio evolved, every decision made sense on its own.

Dashboards.

Analytics.

Creator workflows.

Teacher experiences.

Reporting.

Permissions.

Each addition solved a real problem.

None of them felt unnecessary.

Yet together they introduced a new kind of complexity.

Not because the system was becoming poorly built.

But because the system had become something larger than any single feature.

Another conversation revealed the same pattern.

"Every time we touch one part, we end up worrying about what else it might affect."

Again, this wasn't fear.

It was responsibility.

The team had reached a point where changing one part of the product required confidence in everything connected to it.

The challenge wasn't writing code.

It was preserving understanding.

This is a point many growing products quietly arrive at.

The work no longer revolves around adding the next feature.

Instead, the product begins asking different questions.

Can new people understand how this works?

Can decisions made today still make sense six months from now?

Will today's shortcut become tomorrow's constraint?

Can different teams still share the same mental model of the system?

Those aren't scaling questions.

They're clarity questions.

One of the earliest moments this responsibility surfaced in Plio appeared during a discussion around testing.

The question wasn't whether an individual feature worked.

It became something deeper.

How do we know the system still behaves as expected when a real teacher opens it, on a real browser, under real classroom conditions?

The conversation had shifted.

Testing wasn't about verifying features anymore.

It was about trusting the system.

That changed the nature of the work.

The important conversations slowed down.

Instead of immediately solving problems, more time was spent understanding them.

Instead of asking what should be built next, the team spent time reconnecting every decision back to the larger system.

From the outside, that can feel slower.

In reality, it protects everything that comes after.

Over time, another realization emerged.

Products don't usually become difficult because they grow.

They become difficult when understanding grows more slowly than the product itself.

Every new capability increases the surface area of decisions.

Every new workflow introduces another relationship.

Every new integration creates another dependency.

Growth isn't the problem.

Losing clarity is.

Looking back, that's the shift Plio represents.

The project didn't simply need more engineering.

It needed someone willing to preserve clarity, intent and direction while the product continued to evolve.

Because the goal wasn't simply to help Plio become larger.

The goal was to help it remain understandable.

Even as everything around it changed.

---

# Working Notes

## Philosophy I'm exploring

> "The focus is not on scale. It's on preserving clarity, intent, and direction over time—even as needs change."

## Canonical moments

- "It's not that anything is broken..."
- "Every time we touch one part..."

## Questions I'm exploring

- Does this story naturally make the philosophy visible?
- Am I staying close enough to the client's experience?
- Am I explaining too much instead of letting the story show it?
